#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073565 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a1c72ed5-ab55-5815-b0a2-9a88eb15d362" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing Ratios"}] :question/id #uuid "9a740a80-f655-5d20-a839-d9a809d4c6a4" :question/math-academy-id "e-2285" :question/problem "By finding the missing values in the ratio table below, determine the graph that represents the given relationship.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ |  |\n|  | $6$ |" :question/worked-solution "We start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}4}:{\\color{red}2}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}4}}{{\\color{red}2}}\n$$\n\nWe can generate equivalent ratios by finding equivalent fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 2}{2 \\cdot 2} &= \\frac{8}{4} \\\\\n\\frac{4 \\cdot 3}{2 \\cdot 3} &= \\frac{12}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these to our ratio table:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $4$ | $2$ |\n| $8$ | $4$ |\n| $12$ | $6$ |\n\nSo, we have the following ordered pairs: $(4,2)$, $(8,4)$, $(12,6)$.\n\nPlotting these points on the coordinate plane and connecting them with a straight line, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/8d/8def1b37d62f91a16109db0da3287042fe5893b97bf447e6ee16946b3f8d4bfc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073566 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2f97b-d178-59b0-b58f-b273ddc2ec1d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Ratio From a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "ce72406a-a57e-53b5-b066-f147e197d317" :question/math-academy-id "e-2287" :question/problem "![](images/73/73c3b1e1e419590794caa7614bfc17c686aaae35e84fbb889ac1208a0f3d0ea3.png)\n\nWhat $x:y$ ratio is shown in the graph above?" :question/worked-solution "We see that the point $(32,20)$ lies on the graph. Let's write the ratio $32:20$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{32}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{32 \\div 4}{20 \\div 4} &= \\frac{8}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the graph shows the ratio\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "309e24a1-6c8c-5705-beca-001fed696acc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Ratio From a Graph: Word Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "a0c6e401-4364-575d-94d2-97108b688d43" :question/math-academy-id "e-2289" :question/problem "![](images/33/33a587df3757e82def04bbf4130e0ef681f0902ffc3086dcb6b8882c297f35ad.png)\n\nBetty is making pizza. The amount of water she needs for a given amount of flour is shown in the graph above. In its simplest form, what is the ratio of flour to water?" :question/worked-solution "One of the points belonging to the graph is $(15,6)$.\n\nLet's write the ratio $15:6$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{15}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this fraction by dividing both the numerator and the denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{15 \\div 3}{6 \\div 3} &= \\frac{5}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the ratio of flour to water is $5:2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074429 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2f97b-d178-59b0-b58f-b273ddc2ec1d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Ratio From a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf0c3d66-29a5-54e6-9638-45efb5afce01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072813} {:answer/id #uuid "1f21121f-ef08-59e2-aace-5ed9ec9d145d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072814} {:answer/id #uuid "25c7ed54-b97c-561f-98ae-65f6d6022370" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072815} {:answer/id #uuid "558047f6-99cd-59e6-aa8b-ab05e2dbaa60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072816} {:answer/id #uuid "600f337f-3f3e-5989-85a4-6e3691892d4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072817}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2f7b07ff-5c7c-530b-b30d-b8ffb962eed2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072812}] :question/id #uuid "f5610d75-ae73-55b0-b2d2-e9d14c444b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-60300" :question/problem "![](images/ff/fff2856c61220b283803ca21cacc4a47be8888d75bc6ad35df5f09a1136d4c51.png)\n\nWhat $x: y$ ratio is shown in the graph above?"}]}
